.\" The man page the corpus's man recording shows: written for the purpose.
.TH FUX-CORPUS 1 2026-10-02 "fux-vt-compare" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fux-corpus \- recordings of real programs, replayed through terminal emulators
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fux-vt-compare record
.B \-\-keys
.I file
.B \-\-out
.I prefix
.RB [ \-\-size
.IR rows x cols ]
.B \-\-
.I program
.RI [ args ...]
.br
.B fux-vt-compare corpus
.RB [ \-\-engines
.IR list ]
.RI [ name ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A recording is what a program wrote to its terminal, byte for byte, while
keys were typed into it, one step at a time.
.B record
runs the program on a pseudoterminal as a pane of
.BR fux (1)
runs it, with
.B TERM=xterm-256color
and an environment of its own, and answers its queries as fux would.
.PP
.B corpus
gives each recording to fux-vt and to other engines, and compares their
screens after every step, cell by cell.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-keys " file"
One step a line, written as escapes:
.B \ee
for ESC,
.B \er
for Return,
.BI \ex NN
for any other byte.
A line starting with
.B #
is a comment.
.TP
.BI \-\-size " rows" x cols
The terminal's size; 40 by 120 if not given.
.TP
.BI \-\-scrub " old" = new
Replace
.I old
in the output, before it is saved.
.TP
.BI \-\-engines " list"
Engine names joined by commas, as
.B run
takes them.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I corpus/NAME.bin
The bytes the program wrote, in order.
.TP
.I corpus/NAME.json
What was run, its version, the size, the environment, and each step's keys
with where its output ends.
.SH EXAMPLES
Record
.BR less (1)
paging a file, typing
.B j
twice and then
.BR q :
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
printf \(aqj\enj\enq\en\(aq > less.keys
fux-vt-compare record \-\-keys less.keys \-\-out less \-\- less README.md
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B corpus
exits 0 when every recording expected to agree does,
1 when one does not, and 2 on a usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fux (1),
.BR xterm (1),
.BR less (1)
